    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Tax Forms and Publications</h3>
      <p><strong>4029</strong> Application for Exemption From Social Security and Medicare Taxes</p>
      <p><strong>4361</strong> Application for Exemption From Self-Employment Tax</p>
      <p><strong>4362</strong> Application for Exemption From Self-Employment Tax for Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4040</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4041</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4042</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4043</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4044</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4045</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4046</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4047</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4048</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4049</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4050</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4051</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4052</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4053</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4054</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
      <p><strong>4055</strong> Clergy and Religious Workers</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Employment Status for Other Tax Purposes</h3>
      <p>Even though social security taxes, and other taxes, are not paid by the clergy, the clergy may still be subject to other taxes. These taxes include income tax, self-employment tax, and other taxes.</p>
      <p><strong>Common-law employee.</strong> Depending on all the facts and circumstances, a minister or religious worker may be treated as a common-law employee. If so, the employer is responsible for withholding and paying the taxes. If the clergy is treated as an independent contractor, the clergy is responsible for paying the taxes.</p>
      <p><strong>Independent contractor.</strong> If the clergy is treated as an independent contractor, the clergy is responsible for paying the taxes. The clergy must file a tax return and pay the taxes.</p>
      <p><strong>Self-employed.</strong> If the clergy is self-employed, the clergy is responsible for paying the taxes. The clergy must file a tax return and pay the taxes.</p>
      <p><strong>Example.</strong> A church hires and pays a minister. The minister is an employee. The church withholds and pays the taxes. If the minister is an independent contractor, the minister is responsible for paying the taxes. The minister must file a tax return and pay the taxes.</p>
      <p><strong>Religious order.</strong> If the clergy is a member of a religious order, the religious order may be treated as an employer or an independent contractor. The religious order must determine the correct employment status and pay the taxes accordingly.</p>
      <p><strong>Services covered under FICA at the election of the order.</strong> If you are a member of a religious order, you may be able to elect to have your services covered under FICA. If you make this election, the religious order will pay the taxes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
