  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Regular Method</h3>
      <p>Most people use the regular method. Under this method, you figure out your net earnings from self-employment and then calculate your self-employment tax. You can use Worksheet 1 through 4 in this publication to help you figure out your net earnings.</p>
      <p><strong>Net earnings from self-employment.</strong></p>
      <p>Net earnings from self-employment are your total earnings from self-employment minus your allowable deductions. Allowable deductions include expenses related to your religious duties.</p>
      <p><strong>Self-employment tax.</strong></p>
      <p>The self-employment tax is calculated on your net earnings from self-employment. The tax rate is 15.3%.</p>
      <p><strong>Nonfarm Optional Method</strong></p>
      <p>You may be able to use the nonfarm optional method to figure out your net earnings from self-employment. This method is permitted to permit continued coverage for social security and Medicare under SECA.</p>
      <p>You may use the nonfarm optional method if you meet all the following tests:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>You are self-employed on a regular basis.</li>
        <li>You have at least $400 of net earnings from self-employment.</li>
        <li>You have not taken a vow of poverty.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you meet these tests, you use Table 3 to figure out your net earnings from self-employment under the nonfarm optional method.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Income Tax: Income and Expenses</h3>
      <p>Some income and expenses are treated differently for both the income tax and SE tax purposes.</p>
      <p><strong>Overseas duty.</strong></p>
      <p>Overseas duty is an amount you pay to provide or rent a place of worship or religious facility overseas.</p>
      <p><strong>Fair rental value of parsonage.</strong></p>
      <p>Fair rental value of parsonage is the fair rental value of a parsonage or religious facility that you use for your religious duties.</p>
      <p><strong>Allowable deductions.</strong></p>
      <p>Allowable deductions are expenses related to your religious duties. These expenses must be ordinary and necessary for your religious duties.</p>
      <p><strong>Retirement allowances.</strong></p>
      <p>If you are a retired minister, you may be able to deduct certain retirement allowances from your gross income.</p>
      <p><strong>Accountable plans.</strong></p>
      <p>To be an accountable plan, your employer's reimbursement arrangement must meet certain requirements.</p>
      <p><strong>Nonaccountable plan.</strong></p>
      <p>A nonaccountable plan is one that does not meet the requirements of an accountable plan. Reimbursements under a nonaccountable plan are generally not deductible.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

