  </div>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <div class="section-title">Deduction for SE Tax</div>
      <p>You can deduct one-half of your SE tax if you are self-employed. This deduction is not available if you are an employee.</p>
      <p>This is not a deduction in figuring net earnings from self-employment subject to SE tax.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Income Tax Withholding and Estimated Tax</div>
      <p>The federal income tax is a pay-as-you-go tax. You pay taxes throughout the year, rather than waiting until the end of the year. You may employ withholding to pay taxes throughout the year. You may also estimate your tax liability and pay it throughout the year.</p>
      <p>You are a duly ordained, commissioned, or licensed minister, a member of a religious order, or a religious worker, or a citizen of a religious sect, may be exempt from income tax.</p>
      <p>You may be qualified for self-employment tax exemption if you meet certain requirements. See Publication 570.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <div class="section-title">Filing Your Return</div>
      <p>You must file an income tax return for 2006 if your gross income was at least the amount shown in the third column of Table 4.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Additional requirements.</div>
      <p>Even if your income is less than the amount shown in the third column of Table 4, you must file an income tax return if you are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A self-employed individual.</li>
        <li>An employee who is a member of a religious order or a religious worker.</li>
        <li>A citizen of a religious sect.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Retirement Savings Arrangements</div>
      <p>Retirement savings arrangements are plans that offer you a tax-favored way to save for your retirement. These plans allow you to save money for your retirement with tax advantages. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Retirement plans for the self-employed.</div>
      <p>Self-employed individuals may use certain retirement plans, such as a SEP, SIMPLE, or a SIMPLE plan, you must meet certain requirements to be eligible for these plans. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Individual retirement arrangements (IRAs).</div>
      <p>IRAs are individual retirement arrangements that you can use to save money for your retirement. Generally, you can contribute to an IRA if you have earned income. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>Page 11 of 27 of Publication 517</div>
    <div>16:18 - 22-FEB-2007</div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top: 10px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <div class="section-title">Tax-sheltered annuity plans.</div>
      <p>Church-sponsored plans, such as a church pension plan, are tax-sheltered annuity plans. These plans allow you to save for retirement with tax advantages. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Deductions contributing to tax-sheltered plans.</div>
      <p>Contributions to tax-sheltered plans are generally deductible. However, there are certain limitations on the amount you can contribute. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
      <p>An exception to the above applies if you are a minister or chaplain, and in the exercise of your duties, you are a member of a religious order or a religious worker. In this case, you may be able to deduct contributions to a tax-sheltered plan. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <div class="section-title">Earned Income Credit</div>
      <p>The earned income credit is a credit for certain low-to-moderate-income workers. It is designed to help low-to-moderate-income workers pay their taxes. For more information, see Publication 570.</p>
      <div class="section-title">Comprehensive Review</div>
      <p>Rev. John Michaels is the minister of the First United Church. He is self-employed and has a gross income of $<span data-field="gross_income_amount">{{ gross_income_amount }}</span>. He has ministerial expenses of $<span data-field="ministerial_expenses_amount">{{ ministerial_expenses_amount }}</span>. He also has a tax-free passage allowance of $<span data-field="passage_allowance_amount">{{ passage_allowance_amount }}</span>. He claims a deduction for ministerial expenses of $<span data-field="deduction_amount">{{ deduction_amount }}</span>. He also claims a deduction for health insurance of $<span data-field="health_insurance_amount">{{ health_insurance_amount }}</span>. He reports $<span data-field="total_income_amount">{{ total_income_amount }}</span> as his total income.</p>
      <p>The tax-free passage allowance is not subject to income tax. The ministerial expenses are deductible. The health insurance deduction is also deductible. The total income is calculated as gross income minus deductible expenses.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

